The research stage finished, and the evidence supports your Type 1 call. I wrote only `docs/content-analysis/pipeline-logs/2026-10-28_03-37-43-950_daniela-amodei/evidence.json`. It passes the pipeline's `validateEvidence` check, and nothing was published.

**Result:** ready, Type 1 at medium confidence, Type 2 as the alternative, standard page (not an open case). It has 26 sources (25 opened) and 65 claims. Where the 10-06 run had already recorded a source or claim, I kept its ID.

**Why 1 beats 2:** when a principle costs her something, she explains it by what was right: the Pentagon red lines, no ads despite investor pressure, turning away customers. She never says she was protecting Dario or her staff. Her signature management moves apply to everyone, herself included:
- the 2021 spreadsheet that made every employee give every other one feedback, with completion tracked;
- six-monthly reviews that "includes me and [Dario]";
- "no tolerance" for going behind a colleague's back.

She also says her goal in any job only she can do is to get herself out of it. A Two needs to be needed. In the Isaacson interview she asks whether OpenAI's Pentagon move was "principled" or "opportunistic," and Dario immediately softens it. That one exchange also separates her One from his Six.

**The Two side is on the page, not hidden:** "do-gooder," "supportive manager," "people leader," and following Dario into her first field. So are the points against a rigid One: she's relaxed about her own mistakes, excused the doctors who misdiagnosed her, retired a company value because not everyone loved it, and values changing her mind.

